WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:03.830
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.830 --> 00:00:09.970
I know for a fact that some of you remember
our first video on image translation, which

00:00:09.970 --> 00:00:14.250
was approximately 3 years and 250 episodes
ago.

00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:18.710
This was a technique where we took an input
painting, and a labeling of this image that

00:00:18.710 --> 00:00:24.880
shows what kind of objects are depicted, and
then, we could start editing this labeling,

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:29.529
and out came a pretty neat image that satisfies
these labels.

00:00:29.529 --> 00:00:35.460
Then came pix2pix, another image translation
technique which in some cases, only required

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:40.670
a labeling, a source photo was not required
because these features were learned from a

00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:42.850
large amount of training samples.

00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:48.600
And it could perform really cool things, like
translating a landscape into a map, or sketches

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:50.920
to photos, and more.

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:56.090
Both of these works were absolutely amazing,
and I always say, two more papers down the

00:00:56.090 --> 00:01:00.039
line, and we are going to have much higher
resolution images.

00:01:00.039 --> 00:01:05.890
So, this time, here is the paper that is,
in fact, two more papers down the line.

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:07.610
So let’s see what it can do!

00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:11.100
I advise you that you hold on to your papers
for this one.

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:16.570
The input is again, a labeling which we can
draw ourselves, and the output is a hopefully

00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:20.130
photorealistic image that adheres to these
labels.

00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:25.350
I like how first, only the silhouette of the
rock is drawn, so we have this hollow thing

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:30.810
on the right that is not very realistic, and
then, it is now filled in with the bucket

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:32.850
tool, and, there you go.

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:34.110
It looks amazing.

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:39.950
It synthesizes a relatively high-resolution
image and we finally have some detail in there

00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:40.950
too.

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:46.119
But, of course, there are many possible images
that correspond to this input labeling.

00:01:46.119 --> 00:01:50.200
How do we control the algorithm to follow
our artistic goals?

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:55.170
Well, you remember from the first work I’ve
shown you where we could do that by adding

00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:58.180
an additional image as an input style.

00:01:58.180 --> 00:01:59.909
Well, look at that!

00:01:59.909 --> 00:02:05.500
We don’t even need to engage in that, because
here, we can choose from a set of input styles

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:10.950
that are built into the algorithm and we can
switch between them almost immediately.

00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:16.950
I think the results speak for themselves,
but note that not only the visual fidelity,

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:22.150
but the alignment with the input labels is
also superior to previous approaches.

00:02:22.150 --> 00:02:27.420
Of course, to perform this, we need a large
amount of training data where the inputs are

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:31.099
labels, and the outputs are the photorealistic
images.

00:02:31.099 --> 00:02:33.629
So how do we generate such a dataset?

00:02:33.629 --> 00:02:39.569
Drawing a bunch of labels and asking artists
to fill them in sounds like a crude and expensive

00:02:39.569 --> 00:02:40.569
idea.

00:02:40.569 --> 00:02:45.420
Well, of course, we can do it for free by
thinking the other way around!

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:51.129
Let’s take a set of photorealistic images,
and use already existing algorithms to create

00:02:51.129 --> 00:02:52.780
the labeling for them.

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:58.970
If we can do that, we’ll have as many training
samples as many images we have, in other words,

00:02:58.970 --> 00:03:02.239
more than enough to train an amazing neural
network.

00:03:02.239 --> 00:03:08.550
Also, the main part of the magic in this new
work is using a new kind of layer for normalizing

00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:14.330
information within this neural network that
adapts better to our input data than the previously

00:03:14.330 --> 00:03:16.799
used batch normalization layers.

00:03:16.799 --> 00:03:21.409
This is what makes the outputs more crisp
and does not let semantic information be washed

00:03:21.409 --> 00:03:22.970
away in these images.

00:03:22.970 --> 00:03:27.080
If you have a closer look at the paper in
the video description, you will also find

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:33.239
a nice evaluation section with plenty of comparisons
to previous algorithms and according to the

00:03:33.239 --> 00:03:36.769
authors, the source code will be released
soon as well.

00:03:36.769 --> 00:03:41.900
As soon as it comes out, everyone will be
able to dream up beautiful photorealistic

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:44.560
images and get them out almost instantly.

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:46.769
What a time to be alive!

00:03:46.769 --> 00:03:50.750
If you have enjoyed this episode and would
like to support us, please click one of the

